INT. FLOODED HOTEL LOBBY - NIGHT

A grand ruin. Chandeliers hang in black water to the knees. A reception desk rises like an island. Gunfire rumbles far off.

LIEUTENANT ANNEKE DEVRIES, 30s, helmet cracked, sits on the desk with a bandaged thigh. SERGEANT KOFI MENSAH-BOATENG, 40s, wades in from the stairs, rifle held high.

KOFI
Upstairs is clear.

ANNEKE
Then sit.

He climbs onto the desk beside her. She hands him a canteen. He drinks, wipes his mouth, hands it back.

ANNEKE (CONT'D)
How many left in the company?

KOFI
Eleven.

ANNEKE
Yesterday we were forty.

KOFI
Yesterday was yesterday.

ANNEKE
Tell me they got out.

KOFI
Some got out.

ANNEKE
Kofi.

KOFI
I won't lie to you, Lieutenant.

ANNEKE
You never do. It's very irritating.

A beam crashes somewhere above. Water ripples across the lobby. They both flinch, then settle.

ANNEKE (CONT'D)
When this is over, what will you do?

KOFI
Go home. Sit in my mother's kitchen. Eat until I cannot stand.

ANNEKE
That's all?

KOFI
That's a great deal.

ANNEKE
My mother's kitchen burned in the first week.

KOFI
Then you'll come to mine.

She laughs, a short, cracked sound. He smiles. Another distant shell. They both fall silent.

ANNEKE
Kofi. If I don't make it out--

KOFI
You will.

ANNEKE
If I don't.

KOFI
Then I tell your sister you were a terrible card player.

ANNEKE
Tell her I was the worst. She'll believe that.

KOFI
She will.

ANNEKE
Good.

She leans her head on his shoulder. He does not move. They sit in the dark water and listen to the war go quiet.
